; ======================================================================
;  i2c.pi4 - the BCM2711 BSC (Broadcom Serial Control) I2C master, in
;  readable code. THE ONE FILE ON THE Pi 4 THAT KNOWS THE BSC REGISTERS.
;
;  A QUICK EXAMPLE
;
;    XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/gpio.pi4"   ; PinAlt - see DEPENDENCIES
;    XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/i2c.pi4"
;
;    Procedure Main()
;      Define b.i
;      I2cInit()                          ; mux GPIO 2/3, 100 kHz, enable
;      If I2cProbe($76) = #I2C_OK         ; a BMP280 at 0x76?
;        b = 0
;        I2cWrite($76, @regId, 1)         ; point at register 0xD0
;        I2cRead($76, @b, 1)              ; read the chip-id byte
;      EndIf
;    EndProcedure
;
;  ---- WHAT A "BSC" IS, AND WHY THERE IS NO start()/stop() BELOW --------
;
;  The header I2C bus on a Pi 4 is BSC1 - a Broadcom Serial Control
;  master, "compliant with the Philips I2C bus/interface version 2.1"
;  (BCM2711 ARM Peripherals, section 3.1, p.24). It is a PACKET controller,
;  not a bit-banger: you load the slave address, a direction bit and a byte
;  count, set one Start bit, and the hardware drives the whole framed
;  transaction - START, the address, every data byte with its ACK, and the
;  STOP - on its own. So this file has no I2cStart / I2cStop / I2cAck the
;  way a bit-banged driver would; the transaction primitives ARE I2cWrite,
;  I2cRead and I2cProbe, and the START and STOP are the controller's to
;  place. The datasheet write and read sequences (section 3.3, pp.29-30,
;  written there for 10-bit addressing but identical in shape for 7-bit)
;  are exactly this: set DLEN, set A, set C.ST, poll S.
;
;  ***THIS DRIVER IS SINGLE-MASTER, 7-BIT, ONE TRANSACTION AT A TIME.***
;  That is what the header bus is used for and what can be reasoned about
;  without silicon. See REPEATED START below for the one thing it does NOT
;  do and why that is a deliberate refusal rather than an omission.
;
;  ---- TWO BUSES, ONE DRIVER -------------------------------------------
;
;  The Pi 4 brings out a second I2C bus that has nothing to do with the
;  40-pin header: BSC0 on GPIO 44/45, which is what the DSI display and
;  CSI camera connectors are wired to. Both controllers have the same
;  register file, so this file drives both - I2cSelectBus() moves the
;  base and the pin pair, and every procedure reads them out of globals.
;
;    I2cSelectBus(#I2C_BUS_DISPLAY)     ; BSC0, GPIO 44/45, ALT1
;    I2cUp()                            ; mux and enable THAT controller
;    I2cProbe($45)                      ; the panel MCU on a DSI ribbon
;    I2cSelectBus(#I2C_BUS_HEADER)      ; back to BSC1, GPIO 2/3, ALT0
;
;  The default is the header bus, so a program that never selects gets
;  precisely the driver that existed before this was written. The full
;  argument, and the ALT1-versus-ALT2 trap that makes a good ribbon look
;  like a dead one, is at THE SECOND CONTROLLER below.
;
;  ---- DEPENDENCIES ----------------------------------------------------
;
;  RaspberryPi4/Lib/mailbox.pi4 - for MailboxClockRateMeasured() and
;  MailboxClockRate(). The clock feeding the divider is asked for, not
;  assumed - see THE CLOCK. Same arrangement as PinAlt below: this file
;  does NOT include it, the MAIN file lists it FIRST, and a main file that
;  forgets fails loudly at compile time on an undefined procedure.
;
;  RaspberryPi4/Lib/gpio.pi4  - for PinAlt(). I2cInit() muxes GPIO 2 and 3
;  to ALT0 (SDA1 / SCL1) through PinAlt rather than poking GPFSEL itself,
;  because gpio.pi4 already holds the BCM2711's OUT-OF-ORDER ALT table
;  (ALT0 is FSEL code 4, not 0 - Table 64) in one reviewed place, and
;  re-deriving it here would be a second copy of the one fact most likely
;  to be wrong. House rule 10 (a library never includes a library): this
;  file does NOT XIncludeFile gpio.pi4. The MAIN file lists both, gpio.pi4
;  first, and if it is missing this file fails LOUDLY at compile time as an
;  undefined PinAlt - which is the designed failure, not a silent one.
;
;  a64_barrier() - dsb sy, from RaspberryPi4/Intrinsics/bcm2711_hardware.def,
;  the same barrier gpio.pi4 uses. An intrinsic, target-provided for -t pi4,
;  so it needs no include. See MEMORY ORDERING below.
;
;  ---- WHERE THE ADDRESSES COME FROM (do not guess - rule 21) ----------
;
;  Every number in this file is from BCM2711 ARM Peripherals, Chapter 3
;  (BSC), on disk at RaspberryPi4/Reference/ as both
;  bcm2711-peripherals.pdf and .txt.
;
;  THE BASE, derived not remembered. Section 3.2, p.24 lists the eight BSC
;  masters' bus addresses; BSC1 - the one wired to the 40-pin header - is
;  at bus 0x7e804000. Bus $7Ennnnnn is ARM physical $FEnnnnnn under the
;  Pi 4's Low Peripheral mode (the same translation gpio.pi4's header
;  derives from bcm2711.dtsi's soc ranges and the peripherals PDF section
;  1.2.4), so BSC1 is at ARM $FE804000. This is a DIFFERENT window from the
;  0x7e205xxx block the other BSC masters live in - BSC1 is off on its own,
;  which is why the base here is not $FE205000 + something.
;
;  WHICH PINS. Alternative-function table, p.77: GPIO2 ALT0 = SDA1 and
;  GPIO3 ALT0 = SCL1 - the two header pins (physical 3 and 5) that every
;  Pi I2C device is wired to. Those pins carry 1.8k pull-ups to 3.3V ON THE
;  BOARD (Pi 4 product datasheet), so this driver does NOT enable the
;  BCM2711's internal pulls: the external resistors are the right value and
;  the weak internal ones would only fight them. A bus with NO device and
;  the external pulls present rests high, which is exactly what makes the
;  no-ACK probe below honest rather than phantom - see I2cProbe.
;
;  THE REGISTERS. Table 24, p.24 - offsets from the base:
;    0x00 C     Control       0x10 FIFO  Data FIFO
;    0x04 S     Status        0x14 DIV   Clock Divider
;    0x08 DLEN  Data Length   0x18 DEL   Data Delay
;    0x0c A     Slave Address 0x1c CLKT  Clock Stretch Timeout
;  and section 3.2 p.24: "All accesses are assumed to be 32-bit" - so every
;  access below is PeekN / PokeL, exactly as gpio.pi4 argues for its block.
;
;  ---- .l / N, NOT .i. THE ONE THAT SILENTLY MISCOMPILES ---------------
;
;  Every BSC register is 32 bits. On this 64-bit target a bare Peek/Poke or
;  a .i is EIGHT bytes; a PeekI of S would span S and DLEN and a PokeI of C
;  would write S as well. Registers are written with PokeL (a 32-bit store)
;  and read with PeekN & $FFFFFFFF (PeekN is the UNSIGNED 32-bit read;
;  PeekL sign-extends, so S with bit 31... - none of these fields is that
;  high, but the mask matches gpio.pi4 and costs nothing). This is the same
;  reasoning gpio.pi4's header sets out at length; it is the width of the
;  silicon, not a 32-bit leftover.
;
;  ---- MEMORY ORDERING -------------------------------------------------
;
;  BCM2711 ARM Peripherals section 1.3: a barrier before the first access
;  to a peripheral and after the last, because the AXI fabric can reorder
;  accesses to DIFFERENT peripherals. Each public procedure here is one
;  complete BSC service and takes that literally - a64_barrier() on entry
;  and before returning. WITHIN a transaction, every access is to the SAME
;  peripheral (BSC1), which section 1.3 says is always ordered, so the poll
;  loop does not barrier every register touch.
;
;  ---- THE ERROR MODEL - NACK, CLOCK-STRETCH TIMEOUT, WEDGED BUS -------
;
;  Three ways a transaction can fail, and this driver names all three
;  rather than returning a bare 0/1 (rule 3, loud errors):
;
;    #I2C_NACK    S.ERR (Table 26 bit 8): "Slave has not acknowledged its
;                 address" (or a byte written to it). This is the ordinary
;                 "nothing at that address" answer AND the anti-phantom
;                 guarantee - a probe is "present" ONLY if the addressed
;                 slave drove ACK, never merely because the lines idle high.
;    #I2C_CLKT    S.CLKT (Table 26 bit 9): the slave held SCL low past the
;                 CLKT timeout (Table 32) - it is clock-stretching beyond
;                 what we allowed, i.e. hung or far too slow. The BCM2711
;                 "fixes the clock-stretching bug that was present in
;                 BCM283x devices" (section 3.1), so on THIS part a stretch
;                 is handled in hardware and this bit means a real overrun.
;    #I2C_TIMEOUT our own no-progress deadline: the controller did not
;                 complete or advance a byte within 20 ms. The local
;                 architectural counter gives elapsed time independently
;                 of CPU/cache speed. A finite spin backstop also remains
;                 for an unavailable or stopped counter; that fallback
;                 alone is not a wall-time guarantee. Abort recovery has
;                 its own 20 ms deadline and refuses a new START if TA
;                 never clears. See THE WALL-CLOCK BACKSTOP below.
;
;  ---- REPEATED START: TWO PATHS, AND THE DEFAULT IS STILL TWO STOPS ---
;
;  A register read (write the register pointer, then read the data) is done
;  BY DEFAULT as two separate transactions - an I2cWrite then an I2cRead -
;  which puts a STOP between them (write, STOP, START, read). Most
;  register-style devices (EEPROMs, the common sensors) accept that, and
;  `i2c read` at the command layer still works exactly that way and still
;  says so in its own help text.
;
;  I2cWriteRead() is the other path, added 2026-09-06, and it emits a real
;  repeated start. See its own comment block for the recipe. Use it ONLY
;  for a device that requires Sr; do not "upgrade" the two-transaction
;  callers to it, because a device that is happy with a STOP gains nothing
;  and every extra user of a mid-transaction poke is another thing to
;  re-prove on silicon.
;
;  WHY IT WAS REFUSED BEFORE, AND WHAT CHANGED. The refusal said that the
;  manual's method (section 3.3.2, steps 5-7: start the write, poll S.TA,
;  then rewrite DLEN and set C.READ|C.ST) is a mid-transaction poke of
;  exactly the shape this project has already paid for TWICE on the RP2040
;  and RP2350 (vault: the RP2350 I2C phantom-device fix and the RP2040
;  repeated-start 4805us->126us fix), and that guessing at it with nothing
;  on the bench would be shipping the thing most likely to be subtly wrong.
;  That reasoning still stands and it is why the two-transaction path is
;  untouched. What changed is that a device which REQUIRES Sr arrived - the
;  Goodix GT9xx on the display connector's bus, whose 16-bit register
;  pointer does not survive a STOP - so the choice is no longer "Sr or the
;  simple path", it is "Sr or a driver that reads the wrong register and
;  returns data".
;
;  THE PART THAT IS STILL OWED SILICON is named in I2cWriteRead's own
;  comment: the TA race. It is coded to refuse rather than to read, so the
;  failure mode is a stated error code and not a wrong coordinate.
;
;  ---- STATUS ----------------------------------------------------------
;
;  Written 2026-09-01. COMPILE-VERIFIED ONLY, for -t pi4, as the board.pi4
;  build. NOTHING HERE HAS DRIVEN A REAL BUS. Every register offset and bit
;  is cited against BCM2711 ARM Peripherals Chapter 3 above; the transaction
;  shape follows section 3.3; the anti-phantom argument is the datasheet's
;  own S.ERR definition. But a bus scan against a real device and whether
;  a given sensor tolerates the STOP-between-write-and-read are both still
;  owed a Pi 4. Do not read a green compile as a working bus. (The SCL
;  frequency WAS owed here and is not any more - it was measured on the
;  pin on 2026-09-03; see THE CLOCK.)
;
;  UPDATED 2026-09-03, and the update is a correction. This file always
;  muxed the pins in I2cInit() - the code below is unchanged - but the
;  `i2c probe` and `i2c speed` paths above it never called I2cInit at all,
;  so on real silicon the pins stayed plain inputs, the controller stayed
;  disabled, every transfer spun out, and the monitor blamed the wiring
;  (forum 584). The caller now brings the bus up in one place before any
;  subcommand. Two readers were added here so that claim can be CHECKED
;  rather than assumed: I2cPinsMuxed() reports whether the pads really are
;  on ALT0, and I2cPadLevel() reports what a pad is actually reading, which
;  is what turns "the bus looks wedged" from a guess into a measurement.
;
;  UPDATED AGAIN 2026-09-03, LATER THE SAME DAY (forum 622), and this one
;  is the rate itself. Every number this file printed came out of a
;  datasheet constant of 150 MHz, honestly labelled nominal, while the
;  board next to the label was measuring its core clock at over three
;  times that. So the SCL an operator asked for and the SCL on the wire
;  differed by that ratio, and a request for standard-mode 100 kHz was
;  really putting the line inside fast mode.
;
;  UPDATED 2026-09-06: I2cWriteRead() added, the repeated-start transfer
;  the header above used to say this file did not offer. Built against
;  section 3.3.2 steps 5-7, generalised from the manual's 10-bit recipe to
;  a 7-bit address, with the write bytes pre-loaded into the FIFO the way
;  the recipe does it and wn capped at the FIFO's sixteen. NOTHING ELSE IN
;  THIS FILE CHANGED. It is COMPILE- AND EMULATOR-VERIFIED ONLY; no
;  repeated start from this file has been on a wire. The one race it can
;  lose - the write completing before S.TA is ever observed - returns the
;  new #I2C_NOSR and reads nothing, so if it ever happens on silicon it
;  arrives as a number rather than as a wrong coordinate.
;
;  IT IS MEASURED NOW. The divider's source was established on a wire, by
;  sampling the SCL pad while the controller clocked it at six known
;  dividers (see THE CLOCK), and the driver derives every rate from that
;  clock queried at the time of the call. The nominal constant survives
;  only as a labelled last resort for a firmware that will not answer.
; ======================================================================

EnableExplicit

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE BOARD-SPECIFIC ADDRESS. BSC1, the header bus. bus $7E804000 ->
;  ARM $FE804000 (section 3.2 p.24 + the Low-Peripheral translation).
;  The register constants are OFFSETS inside the block (Table 24), so a
;  missed conversion would not silently write offset $04 for ever - the
;  same discipline gpio.pi4 adopted when it split base from offsets.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#BSC1_BASE     = $FE804000

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE TWO PINS, NAMED ONCE. Alternative-function table, p.77: GPIO2 ALT0
;  is SDA1 and GPIO3 ALT0 is SCL1 - physical header pins 3 and 5. These
;  were spelled as bare 2, 3 and 0 inside I2cInit; they are constants now
;  because three other procedures ask about the same two pins, and three
;  copies of "2" is how a driver ends up muxing one pin and reading the
;  other.
;
;  #I2C_PIN_ALT IS THE ALT NUMBER, NOT THE FSEL CODE. PinAlt() takes 0..5
;  and does the BCM2711's out-of-order mapping itself (ALT0 is FSEL 4, and
;  ALT4 is 3, not 8 - gpio.pi4's Table 64 note). #PIN_ALT0 below is the raw
;  FSEL code, used only where a code read back out of GPFSEL is compared.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#I2C_PIN_SDA   = 2            ; GPIO2, header pin 3 - SDA1 on ALT0
#I2C_PIN_SCL   = 3            ; GPIO3, header pin 5 - SCL1 on ALT0
#I2C_PIN_ALT   = 0            ; the alt NUMBER both pins must be on

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE SECOND CONTROLLER - BSC0, THE ONE THE DISPLAY AND CAMERA
;  CONNECTORS ARE WIRED TO. Same registers, same code, a different base
;  and a different pair of pads.
;
;  This is a SECOND INSTANCE OF THIS LIBRARY, not a second library. Every
;  procedure below reads the base and the two pins out of the globals
;  under THE ACTIVE BUS, and I2cSelectBus() is the only thing that writes
;  them. A copy of this file with three numbers changed would be a
;  divergence, and the two copies would drift on the first bug fix.
;
;  THE BASE. Section 3.2, p.24: BSC0 is at bus 0x7e205000, so ARM
;  $FE205000 under Low Peripheral mode. Confirmed independently by
;  bcm283x.dtsi, which names the same window i2c0: i2c@7e205000.
;
;  WHICH PINS, AND THE ALT THAT IS EASY TO GET WRONG. The DSI and CSI
;  connectors do not reach GPIO 0/1; they reach GPIO 44 and 45, and the
;  Pi device trees mux BSC0 between the two pairs with a pinctrl mux -
;  i2c0mux, states i2c0_gpio0 (GPIO 0/1) and i2c0_gpio44 (GPIO 44/45),
;  rpi-6.12.y_bcm2711-rpi.dtsi:21-30. The display child bus is
;  i2c_csi_dsi: i2c@1, i.e. mux child 1, i.e. GPIO 44/45
;  (rpi-6.12.y_bcm270x.dtsi:125-144).
;
;  ***GPIO 44/45 IS ALT1 FOR BSC0 AND ALT2 FOR BSC1.*** bcm283x.dtsi
;  :197-208 gives both states by name: i2c0_gpio44 is ALT1, i2c1_gpio44
;  is ALT2. Picking the wrong one puts the display connector on the wrong
;  controller, and every transfer then times out with the wiring
;  blameless - the exact shape of forum 584, where pins that were never
;  muxed produced a confident diagnosis about a healthy bus. The alt is a
;  property of the BUS here - I2cBusAlt() derives it from the selector -
;  so it cannot be muxed right for one controller and read back against
;  the other.
;
;  NO EXTERNAL PULL-UPS ARE PROMISED ON THIS PAIR. The 1.8k resistors in
;  the Pi 4 product datasheet are on GPIO 2/3. GPIO 44/45 are pulled up
;  on the DISPLAY BOARD, through the ribbon - so an unplugged or
;  backwards ribbon shows here as both lines LOW, which I2cPadLevel()
;  reads and the caller may print. That is a real diagnostic and it is
;  the cheapest thing on this bench that can tell a bad cable from a bad
;  driver.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#BSC0_BASE     = $FE205000

#I2C_DISP_PIN_SDA = 44        ; GPIO44 - SDA0 on ALT1, the DSI/CSI pair
#I2C_DISP_PIN_SCL = 45        ; GPIO45 - SCL0 on ALT1
#I2C_DISP_PIN_ALT = 1         ; ALT1 for BSC0. ALT2 would be BSC1.

; The bus NUMBERS are the Broadcom controller numbers, so they read the
; same way the datasheet and the device trees do: 1 is the header, 0 is
; the display and camera connectors.
#I2C_BUS_HEADER  = 1
#I2C_BUS_DISPLAY = 0

#BSC_C_OFF     = $00          ; Control
#BSC_S_OFF     = $04          ; Status
#BSC_DLEN_OFF  = $08          ; Data Length (bytes, 16-bit)
#BSC_A_OFF     = $0C          ; Slave Address (7-bit)
#BSC_FIFO_OFF  = $10          ; Data FIFO (8-bit window, 16 deep)
#BSC_DIV_OFF   = $14          ; Clock Divider (CDIV, 16-bit)
#BSC_DEL_OFF   = $18          ; Data Delay (FEDL:REDL)
#BSC_CLKT_OFF  = $1C          ; Clock Stretch Timeout (TOUT, 16-bit)

; C register bits - Table 25, p.25.
#BSC_C_I2CEN   = $8000        ; bit 15  enable the controller
#BSC_C_ST      = $80          ; bit 7   start transfer (one-shot, reads 0)
#BSC_C_CLEAR   = $20          ; bit 5   clear FIFO (one-shot); 5:4, x1 or 1x
#BSC_C_READ    = $01          ; bit 0   1 = read packet, 0 = write packet

; S register bits - Table 26, pp.25-27.
#BSC_S_CLKT    = $200         ; bit 9   clock-stretch timeout (write 1 clears)
#BSC_S_ERR     = $100         ; bit 8   slave did not ACK (write 1 clears)
#BSC_S_RXF     = $80          ; bit 7   FIFO full
#BSC_S_TXE     = $40          ; bit 6   FIFO empty
#BSC_S_RXD     = $20          ; bit 5   FIFO contains data to read
#BSC_S_TXD     = $10          ; bit 4   FIFO has space to write
#BSC_S_RXR     = $08          ; bit 3   FIFO needs reading
#BSC_S_TXW     = $04          ; bit 2   FIFO needs writing
#BSC_S_DONE    = $02          ; bit 1   transfer done (write 1 clears)
#BSC_S_TA      = $01          ; bit 0   transfer active

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE CLOCK. DIV/CDIV, Table 30 p.28: "SCL = core_clock / CDIV. Where
;  core_clk is nominally 150 MHz." So 150 MHz / 1500 = 100 kHz, which is
;  the reset default (0x05dc) and the Pi 4's device-tree i2c1 default
;  (bcm2711-rpi-4-b.dts: clock-frequency = <100000>).
;
;  ***THE 150 MHz IS NOT WHAT THIS PART RUNS AT, AND IT IS NO LONGER USED
;  TO COMPUTE ANYTHING.*** It stays below as a labelled last resort and
;  nothing else. What replaced it is a measurement.
;
;  WHAT WAS MEASURED, 2026-09-03, ON A Pi 4 (forum 622). The divider was
;  programmed to six known values, a transaction was started at each, and
;  the SCL pad was sampled in a tight loop while the controller clocked
;  it; the architectural counter timestamped the first and last falling
;  edge, so SCL came out of an edge count and a tick count and the source
;  came out of SCL * CDIV. Six dividers gave one source clock, and that
;  source is the firmware's MEASURED VideoCore/core clock, at a ratio of
;  one to one. It is NOT 150 MHz and it is NOT the configured figure the
;  same mailbox reports for the same clock. See the diagnostic
;  RaspberryPi4/Examples/Diagnostics/pi4I2cScl.pi4, which is the
;  instrument that settled it and can be re-run on any board.
;
;  SO THE SOURCE IS QUERIED, NOT ASSUMED, AND IT IS QUERIED AT THE TIME
;  OF THE CALL. The core clock is not a constant on this part: the
;  firmware's governor moves it for thermal and load reasons with no
;  notification, and a rate computed from a value cached at boot would
;  drift away from the wire exactly when the board is busy. I2cSourceHz()
;  below asks the firmware every time.
;
;  ---- DEPENDENCY: THE MAILBOX ----------------------------------------
;
;  Asking the firmware means RaspberryPi4/Lib/mailbox.pi4, which this file
;  does NOT include (house rule 10 - a library never includes a library).
;  The MAIN file lists mailbox.pi4 BEFORE this one, exactly as it lists
;  gpio.pi4 before this one for PinAlt; if it does not, the build fails
;  loudly on an undefined MailboxClockRateMeasured, which is the designed
;  failure and not a silent one.
;
;  A firmware that will not answer is not a crash and not a lie: the
;  source falls back to the configured rate, and then to the datasheet
;  nominal, and I2cSourceFrom() says which of the three is in force so a
;  caller can print it. A number whose provenance cannot be stated is the
;  thing this whole topic was about.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#I2C_CORE_NOMINAL_HZ = 150000000 ; datasheet nominal - THE LAST RESORT ONLY
#I2C_DEFAULT_HZ = 100000      ; standard-mode 100 kHz, the Pi 4 default
#I2C_CLKT_TOUT  = $40         ; SCL clocks to allow a stretch (Table 32 reset)

; The divider field's real limits. CDIV is 16 bits and Table 30 says it is
; "always rounded down to an even number", so 65534 is the largest usable
; value and 2 the smallest; 0 is special-cased by the hardware to mean
; 32768 and is never written here. Every rate this driver can and cannot
; reach follows from these two numbers.
#I2C_CDIV_MIN  = 2
#I2C_CDIV_MAX  = 65534

; Where I2cSourceHz() got its answer. Printed by the caller, because "the
; divider is fed from 500000992 Hz" is worth very little without "and that
; is the firmware's measurement of it, taken just now".
#I2C_SRC_MEASURED   = 1       ; the firmware MEASURED the core clock
#I2C_SRC_CONFIGURED = 2       ; the firmware's configured rate; it would
                              ; not give a measurement
#I2C_SRC_NOMINAL    = 3       ; nothing answered - the datasheet constant

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE WALL-CLOCK BACKSTOP. CNTFRQ_EL0/CNTPCT_EL0 give this Pi-specific
;  driver a real elapsed-time bound without a dependency include. Twenty
;  milliseconds is over two hundred 100 kHz bit cells and far beyond one
;  byte or the separately programmed hardware stretch window. Each byte of
;  progress resets the deadline, so a healthy long transfer does not time
;  out merely for being long. The spin count remains an independent fallback
;  for a loader that left CNTFRQ_EL0 at zero.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#I2C_SPIN_MAX  = 2000000
#I2C_PROGRESS_TIMEOUT_US = 20000
#I2C_RECOVER_TIMEOUT_US = 20000

; Result codes - see hal.pi4's #HW_I2C_* portable vocabulary, which these
; equal so the board backend can hand them straight up to the core. Named
; locally too so this library reads on its own.
#I2C_OK        = 0
#I2C_NACK      = -2           ; no device / no acknowledge (S.ERR)
#I2C_CLKT      = -3           ; slave held the clock too long (S.CLKT)
#I2C_TIMEOUT   = -4           ; controller never finished (bus wedged)
#I2C_ARG       = -5           ; bad argument (count <= 0, address > 0x7f)
#I2C_RANGE     = -7           ; a rate the divider cannot reach from the
                              ; clock it is fed by. NOT the same as #I2C_ARG:
                              ; the request was well formed and the answer
                              ; depends on a clock that moves, so the caller
                              ; is owed the reachable range and not a shrug.
                              ; -6 is skipped: the seam above this one spends
                              ; it on "this board has no such bus", and two
                              ; enumerations that agree by accident are how a
                              ; bus error gets relabelled silently.
#I2C_NOSR      = -8           ; the write phase of I2cWriteRead finished
                              ; before the controller ever reported the
                              ; transfer ACTIVE, so the repeated start could
                              ; not be issued and a STOP has already gone out.
                              ; A device that needs Sr has by now forgotten
                              ; its register pointer, so reading anyway would
                              ; return believable rubbish - see the header's
                              ; REPEATED START section. This code has NO
                              ; #HW_I2C_* twin on purpose: no procedure in the
                              ; HwI2c* seam can return it yet, because that
                              ; seam has no write-read call. When one is added,
                              ; hal.pi4 gains the twin and hw_i2c.pi4 maps it
                              ; in the same commit - an unmapped code falling
                              ; through I2cMapCode's default would be reported
                              ; as a timeout, which is the wrong sentence.

; One bounded, non-mutating sequence of controller reads for diagnostics.
; Each member is a 32-bit BSC register read through I2cRd (PeekN); FIFO is
; deliberately absent because reading it consumes received data. The words
; are sequential evidence, not an atomic hardware snapshot: S, DLEN or C can
; change between reads while hardware completes or aborts a transaction. The
; diagnostic starts no transaction and never reads the side-effecting FIFO.
#I2C_SNAP_C = 0
#I2C_SNAP_S = 1
#I2C_SNAP_DLEN = 2
#I2C_SNAP_A = 3
#I2C_SNAP_DIV = 4
#I2C_SNAP_DEL = 5
#I2C_SNAP_CLKT = 6
#I2C_SNAP_WORDS = 7

; Counts transactions that ended in a wedged-bus timeout since boot. A
; timeout is the one failure that suggests wiring rather than a missing
; chip, so it is worth being able to print a running total. Same idea as
; gpio.pi4's gpio_bad_pins.
Global i2c_timeouts.i
Global i2c_recover_failed.i

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE ACTIVE BUS. ONE global, written by I2cSelectBus() and by nothing
;  else, read by every procedure below.
;
;  ***THE ZERO VALUE IS THE HEADER BUS, AND THAT IS NOT A COINCIDENCE.***
;  Globals on this target live in .bss - `global_i2c_sel` in a built image
;  sits at $820xxxx, outside the image entirely - so an initialiser is not
;  data, it is a STORE THAT THE PROGRAM'S STARTUP HAS TO RUN. Every desk
;  gate in tools/a64 calls procedures DIRECTLY, with the program counter
;  set at the entry and no startup anywhere: `a64_anvil_check.py`'s call()
;  zeroes the registers, sets SP and PC and steps. In that world an
;  initialised global reads ZERO.
;
;  So a `Global i2c_base.i = #BSC1_BASE` would have been correct on the
;  board and silently wrong in every gate - I2cInit would have muxed GPIO
;  0 to ALT0 and driven a controller at address zero, and the gate would
;  have failed with a message about pin muxing that pointed nowhere near
;  the cause. Worse, it would have been a defect that only exists off the
;  hardware, which is the hardest kind to believe.
;
;  The selector is therefore an INDEX whose zero value means the bus this
;  file has always driven. #I2C_SEL_HEADER is 0 by definition and the
;  header bus is what an unwritten global selects. Nothing has to run
;  first, on the board or in a model.
;
;  WHY A GLOBAL AND NOT A PARAMETER ON EVERY CALL. A bus argument on
;  I2cWrite would have to be threaded through every caller in the tree,
;  including the ones that will never see a second bus - churn with no
;  reader. And the monitor talks to ONE bus at a time by construction:
;  there is one set of sticky status bits per controller, but there is
;  also one operator typing one command, and a driver that could
;  interleave two buses would need the locking to prove it. This one
;  cannot, and says so, rather than pretending.
;
;  THE STATE THAT IS NOT HERE is the clock divider, deliberately: CDIV
;  lives in each controller's own DIV register, so the two buses keep
;  their own rates in hardware and switching back does not disturb one.
;  i2c_timeouts is shared on purpose - it counts driver events, not bus
;  events, and a running total nobody can attribute is still the number
;  that says something is wrong.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------

; The SELECTOR, which is not the Broadcom bus number. Zero is the header
; bus; see THE ZERO VALUE above. I2cBus() converts to the number the
; datasheet and the device trees use, which is the one a human reads.
#I2C_SEL_HEADER  = 0
#I2C_SEL_DISPLAY = 1

Global i2c_sel.i                  ; 0 = BSC1 on GPIO 2/3, no initialiser

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  I2cAltCode(alt) - the RAW FSEL code for an alt NUMBER, or -1.
;
;  gpio.pi4 owns this mapping and PinAlt() applies it, but PinModeGet()
;  hands back the raw code and there is no public way to ask gpio.pi4 to
;  convert one. The table is Table 64's out-of-order one - ALT4 is 3 and
;  ALT5 is 2 - and it is expressed with the constants gpio.pi4 defines,
;  not with the numbers, so the two cannot drift silently: if gpio.pi4
;  ever renumbers them this file follows without an edit.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i I2cAltCode(alt.i)
  Select alt
    Case 0
      ProcedureReturn #PIN_ALT0
    Case 1
      ProcedureReturn #PIN_ALT1
    Case 2
      ProcedureReturn #PIN_ALT2
    Case 3
      ProcedureReturn #PIN_ALT3
    Case 4
      ProcedureReturn #PIN_ALT4
    Case 5
      ProcedureReturn #PIN_ALT5
  EndSelect
  ProcedureReturn -1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  I2cSelectBus(bus) - point this driver at #I2C_BUS_HEADER (1) or
;  #I2C_BUS_DISPLAY (0). Returns 1, or 0 for a bus this board has not
;  been taught.
;
;  IT DOES NOT BRING THE NEW BUS UP and it does not take the old one
;  down. Selecting is not configuring: the caller runs I2cUp() next, and
;  I2cUp() looks at the bus that is now selected. Doing the mux here
;  would mean a program could not so much as ASK which bus it is on
;  without changing two GPIO pads, and a census has to be able to print
;  the pin states BEFORE it changes them - that is the whole 584 lesson.
;
;  Selecting the bus that is already selected is a no-op, so it is safe
;  on every command line.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i I2cSelectBus(bus.i)
  If bus = #I2C_BUS_HEADER
    i2c_sel = #I2C_SEL_HEADER
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  If bus = #I2C_BUS_DISPLAY
    i2c_sel = #I2C_SEL_DISPLAY
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Which bus is selected, and the numbers that make it that bus. A caller
;  printing a result MUST be able to say which controller and which pads
;  it is talking about, or the result is unattributable.
;
;  Derived from the selector rather than stored beside it, so there is
;  exactly ONE piece of state and no way for four globals to disagree
;  about which bus this is.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i I2cBus()
  If i2c_sel = #I2C_SEL_DISPLAY
    ProcedureReturn #I2C_BUS_DISPLAY
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn #I2C_BUS_HEADER
EndProcedure

Procedure.i I2cBusBase()
  If i2c_sel = #I2C_SEL_DISPLAY
    ProcedureReturn #BSC0_BASE
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn #BSC1_BASE
EndProcedure

Procedure.i I2cBusSda()
  If i2c_sel = #I2C_SEL_DISPLAY
    ProcedureReturn #I2C_DISP_PIN_SDA
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn #I2C_PIN_SDA
EndProcedure

Procedure.i I2cBusScl()
  If i2c_sel = #I2C_SEL_DISPLAY
    ProcedureReturn #I2C_DISP_PIN_SCL
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn #I2C_PIN_SCL
EndProcedure

Procedure.i I2cBusAlt()
  If i2c_sel = #I2C_SEL_DISPLAY
    ProcedureReturn #I2C_DISP_PIN_ALT
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn #I2C_PIN_ALT
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Register access - one 32-bit read, as a positive number, and one
;  32-bit write. No barrier here; the public procedures barrier at their
;  edges (section 1.3), and every access inside a transaction is to this
;  same peripheral, which is ordered.
;
;  THE BASE IS CHOSEN HERE, INLINE, and not through I2cBusBase(). These
;  two run inside I2cXfer's polling loop - the innermost thing this
;  library does - and the loop's backstop counts iterations, so making
;  each one slower lengthens a timeout that is documented in bytes. One
;  compare and a branch costs nothing and keeps that promise. The
;  OFFSETS stay constants: they are the same in every BSC.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i I2cRd(off.i)
  If i2c_sel = #I2C_SEL_DISPLAY
    ProcedureReturn PeekN(#BSC0_BASE + off) & $FFFFFFFF
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn PeekN(#BSC1_BASE + off) & $FFFFFFFF
EndProcedure

Procedure I2cWr(off.i, v.i)
  If i2c_sel = #I2C_SEL_DISPLAY
    PokeL(#BSC0_BASE + off, v)
  Else
    PokeL(#BSC1_BASE + off, v)
  EndIf
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  I2cSourceHz() - the clock feeding CDIV, in Hz, ASKED FOR NOW.
;
;  Three answers in order of honesty, and I2cSourceFrom() records which
;  one was taken so the caller can say it out loud:
;
;    1. the firmware's MEASURED core clock. This is what the divider is
;       fed by - measured on the wire, 2026-09-03, see THE CLOCK - and it
;       is an observation rather than an intention.
;    2. its CONFIGURED core clock, if the measurement is unavailable. The
;       governor moves the real clock away from this, so it is second.
;    3. the datasheet's nominal 150 MHz, if the firmware answers neither.
;       This is the value the whole of forum 622 was about; it survives
;       only as the answer of last resort, and only ever labelled.
;
;  A ZERO from the mailbox is its "I do not know" and is treated as no
;  answer, exactly as mailbox.pi4's header says to.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Global i2c_src_from.i

Procedure.i I2cSourceHz()
  Define hz.i
  hz = MailboxClockRateMeasured(#MBX_CLK_CORE)
  If hz > 0
    i2c_src_from = #I2C_SRC_MEASURED
    ProcedureReturn hz
  EndIf
  hz = MailboxClockRate(#MBX_CLK_CORE)
  If hz > 0
    i2c_src_from = #I2C_SRC_CONFIGURED
    ProcedureReturn hz
  EndIf
  i2c_src_from = #I2C_SRC_NOMINAL
  ProcedureReturn #I2C_CORE_NOMINAL_HZ
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  I2cSourceFrom() - which of the three the last I2cSourceHz() took.
;  Meaningless before the first call, which is why every caller here
;  takes the rate first and asks this second.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i I2cSourceFrom()
  ProcedureReturn i2c_src_from
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE DIVIDER ARITHMETIC. FOUR PURE PROCEDURES, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE.
;
;  None of these reads a register, calls anything or touches a global.
;  They take a source clock and a number and return a number, so the
;  project's own A64 interpreter can execute them on the real built image
;  with a flat memory and no device model - which is what
;  tools/a64/a64_anvil_check.py does with them. The arithmetic that
;  decides what goes on the wire is therefore the arithmetic a gate runs
;  on every build, and the same shape as I2cTimeoutVerdict.
;
;  THE ROUNDING GOES THE SAFE WAY, AND IT CHANGED. The old code took
;  src/hz and truncated, which rounds the DIVIDER down and so pushes the
;  RATE UP - past the rate that was asked for. On a bus that is the wrong
;  direction to be wrong in: a device rated for 100 kHz is not harmed by
;  99,960 Hz and may well be by 100,040. So the divider is rounded UP,
;  then up again to the next even value, and the achieved rate is at or
;  below the requested one, always.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------

; The fastest SCL this divider can produce from that source: CDIV = 2.
Procedure.i I2cRateMax(src.i)
  If src <= 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn src / #I2C_CDIV_MIN
EndProcedure

; And the slowest: CDIV = 65534.
Procedure.i I2cRateMin(src.i)
  If src <= 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn src / #I2C_CDIV_MAX
EndProcedure

; The rate an already-programmed divider gives. CDIV read back as 0 means
; 32768 in the hardware (Table 30), never a divide by zero.
Procedure.i I2cRateFor(src.i, cdiv.i)
  If src <= 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If cdiv = 0
    cdiv = 32768
  EndIf
  If cdiv < 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn src / cdiv
EndProcedure

; The even divider that gives the fastest rate NOT ABOVE hz. Returns 0
; for a nonsense argument; clamps to the field's limits, so a caller that
; wants to refuse an unreachable rate must compare against I2cRateMin and
; I2cRateMax first rather than reading a clamp as a failure.
Procedure.i I2cCdivFor(src.i, hz.i)
  Define cdiv.i
  If src <= 0 Or hz <= 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  cdiv = (src + hz - 1) / hz          ; round the divider UP - see above
  If (cdiv & 1) <> 0
    cdiv = cdiv + 1                   ; Table 30: even, and up not down
  EndIf
  If cdiv < #I2C_CDIV_MIN
    cdiv = #I2C_CDIV_MIN
  EndIf
  If cdiv > #I2C_CDIV_MAX
    cdiv = #I2C_CDIV_MAX
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn cdiv
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  I2cSetDiv(cdiv) / I2cGetDiv() - the register, plainly.
;
;  Separated from the rate so an instrument can program a KNOWN divider
;  and measure what comes out of the pin, which is how the source clock
;  above was established. Nothing in the ordinary path calls I2cSetDiv
;  with a number it did not get from I2cCdivFor.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure I2cSetDiv(cdiv.i)
  a64_barrier()
  I2cWr(#BSC_DIV_OFF, cdiv & $FFFF)
  a64_barrier()
EndProcedure

Procedure.i I2cGetDiv()
  Define cdiv.i
  a64_barrier()
  cdiv = I2cRd(#BSC_DIV_OFF) & $FFFF
  a64_barrier()
  ProcedureReturn cdiv
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  I2cSetSpeed(hz) - program CDIV for the fastest SCL not above hz, from
;  the clock the divider is ACTUALLY fed by right now. Returns the
;  achieved rate in Hz, or:
;
;    #I2C_ARG    hz was not a positive number of hertz
;    #I2C_RANGE  hz is outside what the divider can reach from that clock
;
;  A REFUSAL RATHER THAN A CLAMP, and that is the point of the new code.
;  Asking for 2 kHz on a bus whose slowest is 7.6 kHz used to silently
;  give 7.6 kHz and report it as if it had been asked for. The caller now
;  gets #I2C_RANGE and can name the nearest reachable rate from
;  I2cRateMin / I2cRateMax, which are computed from the same source in
;  the same call.
;
;  The DEL register's edge delays must stay below CDIV/2 (Table 31 note);
;  the reset DEL (0x0030:0x0030) is 48, which is below CDIV/2 for every
;  CDIV from 98 upwards - so it holds for any rate this driver will
;  program below source/98, and the fast end of the range is where a
;  future DEL adjustment would belong. Left at reset, and said out loud.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i I2cSetSpeed(hz.i)
  Define cdiv.i
  Define src.i
  If hz <= 0
    ProcedureReturn #I2C_ARG
  EndIf
  src = I2cSourceHz()
  If hz > I2cRateMax(src) Or hz < I2cRateMin(src)
    ProcedureReturn #I2C_RANGE
  EndIf
  cdiv = I2cCdivFor(src, hz)
  If cdiv <= 0
    ProcedureReturn #I2C_ARG
  EndIf
  I2cSetDiv(cdiv)
  ProcedureReturn I2cRateFor(src, cdiv)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  I2cGetSpeed() - the SCL the divider is currently set to, computed from
;  the source clock as it is NOW. Two calls a second apart can honestly
;  differ without the divider having been touched, because the governor
;  moves the clock underneath it; that is a property of this part and not
;  a defect in this procedure.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i I2cGetSpeed()
  ProcedureReturn I2cRateFor(I2cSourceHz(), I2cGetDiv())
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  I2cInit() - mux the header pins, set the clock and stretch timeout,
;  enable the controller. Idempotent; safe to call more than once.
;
;  PinAlt mux GPIO 2 and 3 to ALT0 (SDA1 / SCL1). This is the documented
;  dependency on gpio.pi4; if that library is not linked ahead of this one
;  the build fails on PinAlt, which is the point.
;
;  ***CALLING THIS IS NOT OPTIONAL AND IT IS NOT A PERFORMANCE CHOICE.***
;  Until these two PinAlt calls have run, GPIO 2 and 3 are whatever the
;  firmware left them as - on a Pi 4 that is plain INPUTS - and BSC1's SDA
;  and SCL are connected to nothing at all. The controller then drives a
;  transaction into a disconnected bus, never sees DONE, and I2cXfer times
;  out. That timeout is indistinguishable at the register level from a bus
;  whose lines are shorted low, which is exactly how forum 584 happened:
;  every subcommand that skipped this procedure got a confident wiring
;  diagnosis on a bus that was measurably idle-high and healthy. The pull
;  state is deliberately left as the board provides it - the Pi 4 carries
;  1.8k external pull-ups on these two pins and the weak internal ones
;  would only fight them (see WHICH PINS in the header).
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i I2cInit()
  Define r.i
  ; The SELECTED bus's pads and the SELECTED bus's alt. On bus 1 that is
  ; GPIO 2/3 to ALT0 (SDA1/SCL1); on bus 0 it is GPIO 44/45 to ALT1
  ; (SDA0/SCL0), the display connector's pair - and ALT1 is the number
  ; that must not become ALT2, see THE SECOND CONTROLLER.
  PinAlt(I2cBusSda(), I2cBusAlt())
  PinAlt(I2cBusScl(), I2cBusAlt())
  a64_barrier()
  I2cWr(#BSC_CLKT_OFF, #I2C_CLKT_TOUT)
  ; 100 kHz from whatever the divider is really fed by. A source that
  ; cannot reach 100 kHz at all would be an extraordinary board, but the
  ; refusal is a real return value now, so it is handled rather than
  ; discarded: the divider is left at the slowest the field allows, which
  ; is the safe end for a bus whose devices are unknown.
  r = I2cSetSpeed(#I2C_DEFAULT_HZ)    ; barriers internally
  If r < 0
    I2cSetDiv(#I2C_CDIV_MAX)
  EndIf
  a64_barrier()
  I2cWr(#BSC_C_OFF, #BSC_C_I2CEN)     ; enable, idle (READ=0, ST=0)
  a64_barrier()
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  I2cIsUp() - is this bus ALREADY up? 1 yes, 0 no.
;
;  Up means both things, not either: the two pads are on the
;  controller's function AND the controller is enabled. Half of that is
;  the 584 shape - a controller driving pins it is not connected to, or
;  pins wired to a controller that is switched off - and neither half
;  alone is a bus.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i I2cIsUp()
  Define c.i
  If I2cPinsMuxed() <> 1
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  a64_barrier()
  c = I2cRd(#BSC_C_OFF)
  a64_barrier()
  If (c & #BSC_C_I2CEN) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  I2cUp() - bring the bus up IF IT IS NOT ALREADY UP. Returns 1.
;
;  ***THIS IS NOT THE SAME AS CALLING I2cInit() TWICE, AND THE DIFFERENCE
;  IS A RATE THE OPERATOR SET.*** I2cInit programs the divider to the
;  100 kHz default - that is its job, it is how a bus comes up - so a
;  caller that runs it before every operation quietly throws away any
;  rate that was chosen since. Found on silicon 2026-09-03 while proving
;  forum 622: `i2c speed 400000` really did put 400 kHz on the wire, and
;  the very next `i2c` command line brought the bus "up" again and put it
;  back to 100 kHz, with the command then honestly reporting 100 kHz. Two
;  true statements and a setting that evaporated between them.
;
;  So: if the bus is up, leave it exactly as it is. If it is not, bring
;  it up. The Arduino UNO Q's backend already made this distinction for
;  the same reason ("already configured; leave the rate alone"); this
;  board did not, and that asymmetry is what made it hard to see.
;
;  I2cInit() keeps its old meaning for a caller that really does want the
;  bus reset to defaults - it is just no longer what the command path
;  runs on every line.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i I2cUp()
  If I2cIsUp() = 1
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn I2cInit()
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  I2cPinMuxed(pin) - is that pin on ALT0 right now? 1 yes, 0 no, -1 for
;  a pin this board does not have.
;
;  PinModeGet returns the RAW 3-bit FSEL code, so the comparison is
;  against #PIN_ALT0 (which is 4 - Table 64 is out of order) and NOT
;  against #I2C_PIN_ALT, which is the alt NUMBER PinAlt takes. Getting
;  those two the wrong way round would compare 0 against 4 and report
;  every muxed pin as unmuxed, so they are named differently on purpose.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i I2cPinMuxed(pin.i)
  Define m.i
  Define want.i
  m = PinModeGet(pin)
  If m < 0
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  ; The code wanted is the SELECTED bus's alt, translated. On bus 1 that
  ; is #PIN_ALT0 (4), exactly as before; on bus 0 it is #PIN_ALT1 (5).
  ; Comparing against a fixed #PIN_ALT0 would report every correctly
  ; muxed display pin as unmuxed, which is a lie in the direction that
  ; makes people re-seat cables.
  want = I2cAltCode(I2cBusAlt())
  If want < 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If m = want
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  I2cPinsMuxed() - 1 only if BOTH bus pins are on ALT0, else 0.
;  ONE pin muxed is not a working bus; it is a half-configured one, and
;  it must not read as ready.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i I2cPinsMuxed()
  If I2cPinMuxed(I2cBusSda()) <> 1
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If I2cPinMuxed(I2cBusScl()) <> 1
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  I2cPadLevel(pin) - the level AT THE PAD: 1 high, 0 low, -1 for a bad
;  pin. DigitalRead reads GPLEV, which on the BCM2711 reflects the pad
;  whatever FSEL says (section 5.2, Tables 74-75) - so this reads a line
;  the BSC is driving just as well as one left as an input, and that is
;  precisely what it is for.
;
;  WHY THIS EXISTS. An I2C bus at rest is HIGH on both lines, held there
;  by pull-up resistors. A transfer that never completes on a bus reading
;  high has NOT got stuck lines and NOT got missing pull-ups, whatever the
;  message used to say; a transfer that never completes with either line
;  low genuinely has. One register read separates the two, and until this
;  procedure existed nothing read it.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i I2cPadLevel(pin.i)
  ProcedureReturn DigitalRead(pin)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  I2cRecover() - put the controller back the way a fresh one would be
;  found, after a transfer that never finished.
;
;  ***WHY THIS EXISTS: MEASURED ON SILICON 2026-09-06, ON BSC0.*** Three
;  identical probe commands in a row answered, timed out, answered -
;  exactly alternating. The transfer that fails is not the one that is
;  broken. Every run ended with a read that never completed; the sticky
;  status bits are cleared BEFORE a transfer and nothing put the
;  controller back after one that did not finish, so the wreckage was
;  handed to the next command, which failed, and whose own failure left
;  the controller in the state the one after that could use.
;
;  ***A DRIVER WHOSE FAILURES ALTERNATE IS WORSE THAN ONE THAT FAILS
;  EVERY TIME.*** Every second run is green, so a bench session calls it
;  intermittent, blames the cable, and a retry loop hides it - while half
;  the readings it produces come off a controller in a state nobody
;  described. That is why this is a fix in the driver and not a retry at
;  the call site.
;
;  What it does follows both the datasheet's controller definition and the
;  upstream BCM2835 driver's timeout path. First write C with CLEAR and
;  I2CEN OFF. That disables the controller and aborts an active transfer;
;  CLEAR also empties both FIFOs. The abort is asynchronous on the bus, so
;  poll TA until the controller says the transfer is no longer active.
;  Only THEN clear DONE, ERR and CLKT: clearing them before TA falls permits
;  a late abort-completion DONE to become the next transaction's status.
;  Finally leave C enabled and idle. Linux uses the same disabled CLEAR at
;  timeout and completion (drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-bcm2835.c:338-342,
;  372-375 at commit 50d05c7c76c96b90462f24debacca971d2e86713).
;
;  It does NOT touch the divider, the slave address or the stretch timeout -
;  a recovery that also reset the rate would throw away a rate the operator
;  chose, which is the mistake I2cUp exists to avoid.
;
;  A single framed I2cXfer clears a reported NACK or clock-stretch status and
;  returns it directly. The combined repeated-start procedure calls recovery
;  for those errors because they can occur between its two phases, while the
;  controller still owns a half-transaction. Recovery never changes the
;  error returned to the caller, so NACK, CLKT and timeout remain distinct.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i I2cRecover()
  Define s.i
  Define spin.i
  Define deadline.i

  a64_barrier()
  I2cWr(#BSC_C_OFF, #BSC_C_CLEAR)

  spin = #I2C_SPIN_MAX
  deadline = I2cDeadlineUs(#I2C_RECOVER_TIMEOUT_US)
  Repeat
    s = I2cRd(#BSC_S_OFF)
    If (s & #BSC_S_TA) = 0
      Break
    EndIf
    spin = spin - 1
  Until spin <= 0 Or I2cDeadlinePassed(deadline) <> 0

  If (s & #BSC_S_TA) <> 0
    ; Leave the controller disabled. A caller already returning an error must
    ; not advertise an enabled-idle controller that never became idle.
    i2c_recover_failed = 1
    a64_barrier()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ; DONE may have arrived as the abort completed. This clear belongs after
  ; the TA observation above, never immediately after requesting the abort.
  I2cWr(#BSC_S_OFF, #BSC_S_DONE | #BSC_S_ERR | #BSC_S_CLKT)
  I2cWr(#BSC_C_OFF, #BSC_C_I2CEN)
  i2c_recover_failed = 0
  a64_barrier()
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; Architectural counter access stays local so this hardware library does not
; include another library. The counter is fixed-frequency across cache/CPU
; regimes; zero frequency activates the independent spin fallback.
Procedure.i I2cCounterHz()
  ASM
    mrs x0, cntfrq_el0
  EndASM
EndProcedure

Procedure.i I2cCounterTicks()
  ASM
    mrs x0, cntpct_el0
  EndASM
EndProcedure

Procedure.i I2cDeadlineUs(us.i)
  Define hz.i
  hz = I2cCounterHz()
  If hz <= 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn I2cCounterTicks() + (hz / 1000000) * us
EndProcedure

Procedure.i I2cDeadlinePassed(deadline.i)
  If deadline = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ; The signed delta is wrap-safe for deadlines less than 2^63 ticks away.
  If (I2cCounterTicks() - deadline) >= 0
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; Refuse to launch a fresh transaction over one that recovery could not make
; idle. The common success path is one status read. A previous failed abort
; leaves I2CEN off; only a confirmed TA-low recovery may enable it again.
Procedure.i I2cEnsureIdle()
  If i2c_recover_failed = 0 And (I2cRd(#BSC_S_OFF) & #BSC_S_TA) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn I2cRecover()
EndProcedure

Procedure.i I2cSnapshot(*snap)
  If *snap = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  PokeL(*snap + #I2C_SNAP_C * 4, I2cRd(#BSC_C_OFF))
  PokeL(*snap + #I2C_SNAP_S * 4, I2cRd(#BSC_S_OFF))
  PokeL(*snap + #I2C_SNAP_DLEN * 4, I2cRd(#BSC_DLEN_OFF))
  PokeL(*snap + #I2C_SNAP_A * 4, I2cRd(#BSC_A_OFF))
  PokeL(*snap + #I2C_SNAP_DIV * 4, I2cRd(#BSC_DIV_OFF))
  PokeL(*snap + #I2C_SNAP_DEL * 4, I2cRd(#BSC_DEL_OFF))
  PokeL(*snap + #I2C_SNAP_CLKT * 4, I2cRd(#BSC_CLKT_OFF))
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  I2cXfer(addr, *buf, n, isRead) - ONE framed transaction. The engine
;  behind I2cWrite / I2cRead / I2cProbe. Returns #I2C_OK or an error code.
;
;  The shape is the datasheet's (section 3.3, generalised to 7-bit):
;    clear the sticky S bits, set A, set DLEN, then one write to C with
;    I2CEN + CLEAR + ST (+ READ for a read). Then poll S: feed or drain the
;    FIFO as TXD/RXD allow, watch ERR and CLKT, and stop on DONE.
;
;  ERR AND CLKT ARE CHECKED BEFORE DONE each spin, because a NACK is the
;  answer we most want to report precisely and it can be set in the same
;  status word as DONE. The spin backstop is reset on every byte of
;  progress (see THE WALL-CLOCK BACKSTOP), so it bounds the wait per byte.
;
;  addr is a 7-bit address (0..0x7f). n must be >= 1 - a zero-length
;  transfer is refused here (#I2C_ARG); I2cProbe does its no-data check
;  with a 1-byte read, not a zero-length transfer, because zero-length
;  transfers on this controller family are exactly the unreliable corner
;  the RP2040/RP2350 history warns about.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i I2cXfer(addr.i, *buf, n.i, isRead.i)
  Define s.i
  Define i.i
  Define ctl.i
  Define spin.i
  Define deadline.i

  If n < 1 Or addr < 0 Or addr > $7F
    ProcedureReturn #I2C_ARG
  EndIf
  If I2cEnsureIdle() = 0
    ProcedureReturn #I2C_TIMEOUT
  EndIf

  a64_barrier()
  ; Clear any leftover ERR / CLKT / DONE from a previous transaction, so
  ; this one's status is its own (all three are write-1-to-clear).
  I2cWr(#BSC_S_OFF, #BSC_S_ERR | #BSC_S_CLKT | #BSC_S_DONE)
  I2cWr(#BSC_A_OFF, addr & $7F)
  I2cWr(#BSC_DLEN_OFF, n & $FFFF)

  ctl = #BSC_C_I2CEN | #BSC_C_CLEAR | #BSC_C_ST
  If isRead <> 0
    ctl = ctl | #BSC_C_READ
  EndIf
  I2cWr(#BSC_C_OFF, ctl)

  i = 0
  spin = #I2C_SPIN_MAX
  deadline = I2cDeadlineUs(#I2C_PROGRESS_TIMEOUT_US)
  Repeat
    s = I2cRd(#BSC_S_OFF)

    ; --- the two hardware failures, most specific first ---------------
    If (s & #BSC_S_ERR) <> 0
      I2cWr(#BSC_S_OFF, #BSC_S_ERR)   ; clear it
      a64_barrier()
      ProcedureReturn #I2C_NACK
    EndIf
    If (s & #BSC_S_CLKT) <> 0
      I2cWr(#BSC_S_OFF, #BSC_S_CLKT)
      a64_barrier()
      ProcedureReturn #I2C_CLKT
    EndIf

    ; --- move a byte if the FIFO is ready and there is one to move ----
    If isRead <> 0
      If i < n And (s & #BSC_S_RXD) <> 0
        PokeA(*buf + i, I2cRd(#BSC_FIFO_OFF) & $FF)
        i = i + 1
        spin = #I2C_SPIN_MAX          ; progress - reset the backstop
        deadline = I2cDeadlineUs(#I2C_PROGRESS_TIMEOUT_US)
        Continue
      EndIf
    Else
      If i < n And (s & #BSC_S_TXD) <> 0
        I2cWr(#BSC_FIFO_OFF, PeekA(*buf + i) & $FF)
        i = i + 1
        spin = #I2C_SPIN_MAX
        deadline = I2cDeadlineUs(#I2C_PROGRESS_TIMEOUT_US)
        Continue
      EndIf
    EndIf

    ; --- finished? ----------------------------------------------------
    If (s & #BSC_S_DONE) <> 0
      If isRead <> 0
        ; DONE can be set with bytes still sitting in the FIFO - drain
        ; whatever is left before declaring the count short.
        While i < n And (I2cRd(#BSC_S_OFF) & #BSC_S_RXD) <> 0
          PokeA(*buf + i, I2cRd(#BSC_FIFO_OFF) & $FF)
          i = i + 1
        Wend
      EndIf
      I2cWr(#BSC_S_OFF, #BSC_S_DONE)
      a64_barrier()
      If i < n
        ; Controller says done but we did not move every byte. Not a NACK
        ; and not a stretch - treat as a wedged/short transfer so it is
        ; never mistaken for success.
        i2c_timeouts = i2c_timeouts + 1
        I2cRecover()
        ProcedureReturn #I2C_TIMEOUT
      EndIf
      ProcedureReturn #I2C_OK
    EndIf

    ; --- the wall-clock backstop -------------------------------------
    spin = spin - 1
    If spin <= 0 Or I2cDeadlinePassed(deadline) <> 0
      a64_barrier()
      i2c_timeouts = i2c_timeouts + 1
      I2cRecover()
      ProcedureReturn #I2C_TIMEOUT
    EndIf
  ForEver
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  I2cWrite(addr, *buf, n) - write n bytes to the device. One transaction:
;  START, address+W, the bytes, STOP. Returns #I2C_OK or an error code.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i I2cWrite(addr.i, *buf, n.i)
  ProcedureReturn I2cXfer(addr, *buf, n, 0)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  I2cRead(addr, *buf, n) - read n bytes from the device. One transaction:
;  START, address+R, n bytes in, STOP. Returns #I2C_OK or an error code.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i I2cRead(addr.i, *buf, n.i)
  ProcedureReturn I2cXfer(addr, *buf, n, 1)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  I2cWriteRead(addr, *wbuf, wn, *rbuf, rn) - ONE transaction with a
;  REPEATED START in the middle: START, address+W, wn bytes, Sr,
;  address+R, rn bytes, STOP. No STOP between the two halves.
;
;  Returns #I2C_OK or an error code.
;
;  ---- WHY THIS EXISTS NOW, WHEN THE HEADER SAYS IT DOES NOT -----------
;
;  It said: "A device that REQUIRES a repeated START ... will misbehave,
;  and this driver does not offer it", and named section 3.3.2 as the
;  reference to build this against when there is a Pi 4 to test on. There
;  is one, and there is now a device that requires Sr: the Goodix GT9xx
;  touch controller on the display connector's bus. Its register pointer
;  is 16 bits, written as two bytes, and A STOP AFTER THOSE TWO BYTES
;  MAKES IT FORGET THEM - the subsequent read then returns whatever the
;  pointer happens to be aimed at, which is real data at a wrong address
;  and looks exactly like a working driver reading a wrong-looking panel.
;  RaspberryPi4/Lib/touch_goodix.pi4's header carries the same warning
;  from the other side, and RP2350/Lib/gt911.pico2:372-400 hit it first on
;  a different part.
;
;  The two-transaction path stays exactly as it was and is still the right
;  call for an EEPROM or a common sensor. This is the addition, not a
;  replacement, and I2cWrite / I2cRead are untouched.
;
;  ---- THE RECIPE IS THE UPSTREAM DRIVER'S POLLED EQUIVALENT ------------
;
;  BCM2711 ARM Peripherals section 3.3.2 "Reading" (page 30) writes the
;  procedure out for a 10-bit address, where the low eight bits of the
;  address are themselves the "write" phase. Steps 5 to 7 are the part
;  that matters and they are address-width agnostic:
;
;     5. Poll the I2CS.TA bit, waiting for the transfer to start
;     6. Write the number of data bytes to read to the I2CDLEN register
;     7. Set I2CC.READ = 1 and I2CC.ST = 1, this will send the repeat
;        start bit, the slave address and the R/W bit (which is 1),
;        initiating the read
;
;  There is a silicon-specific catch which that recipe does not make
;  obvious. Linux's BCM2835/BCM2711 driver documents the controller state
;  machine workaround and does NOT preload the FIFO. It starts the write
;  empty, lets TXW prove the transfer is active, fills as much as TXD
;  permits, and queues the read immediately when the last write byte has
;  been put in the FIFO. Primary implementation reference:
;
;    Linux 50d05c7c76c96b90462f24debacca971d2e86713
;    drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-bcm2835.c:209-300
;    https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/50d05c7c76c96b90462f24debacca971d2e86713/drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-bcm2835.c#L209-L300
;
;  Waiting only for TA after PRELOADING is not equivalent. It can ask for
;  READ before the controller has entered the TXW hold which makes the
;  repeated-start transition reliable. That exact old sequence timed out
;  on the Pi 4 display bus while ordinary STOP-separated MCU transfers
;  still worked.
;
;  ---- WHY wn IS NOT CAPPED AT THE FIFO DEPTH --------------------------
;
;  TXW/TXD are the flow control. Long writes refill the FIFO on later TXW
;  observations; short Goodix register pointers fit on the first one. The
;  read is queued in the SAME TXW handling step that enqueues the last byte,
;  just as the upstream interrupt handler does. Waiting for a second TXW or
;  FIFO-empty indication can lose the active window and emit a STOP.
;
;  ---- THE ONE RACE, AND IT IS NAMED RATHER THAN IGNORED ---------------
;
;  If the write phase were to finish entirely before we observe TXW, the
;  controller would have emitted a STOP and step 7 would begin a fresh
;  transaction - a plain read, with no Sr, which is precisely the silent
;  wrong answer this procedure exists to prevent. So the TXW wait ALSO
;  watches DONE, and DONE-before-the-read returns #I2C_NOSR and reads
;  nothing. DONE is checked before TXW, matching the upstream handler.
;
;  At 100 kHz a two-byte write takes about 270 microseconds and the poll
;  is a handful of nanoseconds, so this should never fire. "Should never"
;  is the reason it returns its own code instead of sharing one: if it
;  ever does fire, the number says so, and nobody spends an afternoon on a
;  touch panel that reads the wrong register.
;
;  A NACK on the write phase is NOT this case - a NACK sets S.ERR, which
;  is checked first, and comes back as #I2C_NACK. Every combined-transfer
;  error clears the FIFO/controller transaction state with I2cRecover;
;  that preserves A, DIV and CLKT, including the selected slave address.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i I2cWriteRead(addr.i, *wbuf, wn.i, *rbuf, rn.i)
  Define s.i
  Define i.i
  Define spin.i
  Define deadline.i

  If wn < 1 Or rn < 1 Or addr < 0 Or addr > $7F
    ProcedureReturn #I2C_ARG
  EndIf
  If I2cEnsureIdle() = 0
    ProcedureReturn #I2C_TIMEOUT
  EndIf

  a64_barrier()
  I2cWr(#BSC_S_OFF, #BSC_S_ERR | #BSC_S_CLKT | #BSC_S_DONE)
  I2cWr(#BSC_A_OFF, addr & $7F)

  ; Start with an EMPTY FIFO. Linux's BCM2835 controller driver names this
  ; as part of the repeated-start state-machine workaround. CLEAR is written
  ; without ST and is not repeated at the phase switch.
  I2cWr(#BSC_C_OFF, #BSC_C_I2CEN | #BSC_C_CLEAR)

  I2cWr(#BSC_DLEN_OFF, wn & $FFFF)
  i = 0
  I2cWr(#BSC_C_OFF, #BSC_C_I2CEN | #BSC_C_ST)

  ; The first TXW proves the un-prefilled transfer is active. Each TXW may
  ; refill a partial FIFO. When the final byte is enqueued, queue READ|ST in
  ; this same iteration - do not wait for another status transition.
  spin = #I2C_SPIN_MAX
  deadline = I2cDeadlineUs(#I2C_PROGRESS_TIMEOUT_US)
  Repeat
    s = I2cRd(#BSC_S_OFF)
    If (s & #BSC_S_ERR) <> 0
      I2cRecover()
      ProcedureReturn #I2C_NACK
    EndIf
    If (s & #BSC_S_CLKT) <> 0
      I2cRecover()
      ProcedureReturn #I2C_CLKT
    EndIf
    If (s & #BSC_S_DONE) <> 0
      ; The write is over and a STOP went with it. Do NOT begin a plain read.
      I2cRecover()
      ProcedureReturn #I2C_NOSR
    EndIf

    If (s & #BSC_S_TXW) <> 0
      While i < wn
        s = I2cRd(#BSC_S_OFF)
        If (s & #BSC_S_ERR) <> 0
          I2cRecover()
          ProcedureReturn #I2C_NACK
        EndIf
        If (s & #BSC_S_CLKT) <> 0
          I2cRecover()
          ProcedureReturn #I2C_CLKT
        EndIf
        If (s & #BSC_S_DONE) <> 0
          I2cRecover()
          ProcedureReturn #I2C_NOSR
        EndIf
        If (s & #BSC_S_TXD) = 0
          Break
        EndIf
        I2cWr(#BSC_FIFO_OFF, PeekA(*wbuf + i) & $FF)
        i = i + 1
        spin = #I2C_SPIN_MAX
        deadline = I2cDeadlineUs(#I2C_PROGRESS_TIMEOUT_US)
      Wend

      If i = wn
        I2cWr(#BSC_DLEN_OFF, rn & $FFFF)
        I2cWr(#BSC_C_OFF, #BSC_C_I2CEN | #BSC_C_ST | #BSC_C_READ)
        Break
      EndIf
    EndIf

    spin = spin - 1
    If spin <= 0 Or I2cDeadlinePassed(deadline) <> 0
      i2c_timeouts = i2c_timeouts + 1
      I2cRecover()
      ProcedureReturn #I2C_TIMEOUT
    EndIf
  ForEver

  ; From here it is I2cXfer's read loop, and it is written out rather than
  ; called because I2cXfer owns the whole transaction from its own START.
  i = 0
  spin = #I2C_SPIN_MAX
  deadline = I2cDeadlineUs(#I2C_PROGRESS_TIMEOUT_US)
  Repeat
    s = I2cRd(#BSC_S_OFF)

    If (s & #BSC_S_ERR) <> 0
      I2cRecover()
      ProcedureReturn #I2C_NACK
    EndIf
    If (s & #BSC_S_CLKT) <> 0
      I2cRecover()
      ProcedureReturn #I2C_CLKT
    EndIf

    If i < rn And (s & #BSC_S_RXD) <> 0
      PokeA(*rbuf + i, I2cRd(#BSC_FIFO_OFF) & $FF)
      i = i + 1
      spin = #I2C_SPIN_MAX
      deadline = I2cDeadlineUs(#I2C_PROGRESS_TIMEOUT_US)
      Continue
    EndIf

    If (s & #BSC_S_DONE) <> 0
      While i < rn And (I2cRd(#BSC_S_OFF) & #BSC_S_RXD) <> 0
        PokeA(*rbuf + i, I2cRd(#BSC_FIFO_OFF) & $FF)
        i = i + 1
      Wend
      If i < rn
        i2c_timeouts = i2c_timeouts + 1
        I2cRecover()
        ProcedureReturn #I2C_TIMEOUT
      EndIf
      I2cWr(#BSC_S_OFF, #BSC_S_DONE)
      a64_barrier()
      ProcedureReturn #I2C_OK
    EndIf

    spin = spin - 1
    If spin <= 0 Or I2cDeadlinePassed(deadline) <> 0
      i2c_timeouts = i2c_timeouts + 1
      I2cRecover()
      ProcedureReturn #I2C_TIMEOUT
    EndIf
  ForEver
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  I2cProbe(addr) - is a device answering at this 7-bit address?
;
;  Returns #I2C_OK (present - the slave drove ACK on its address), #I2C_NACK
;  (absent - no ACK), or #I2C_CLKT / #I2C_TIMEOUT for a bus fault. It does a
;  1-byte READ: for a read the only thing that can NACK is the ADDRESS
;  (there is no data written to the slave for it to reject - Table 26 bit 8
;  is "did not acknowledge its address, or a byte written to it"), so a
;  clean 1-byte read cleanly means "the address was acknowledged". The byte
;  read is discarded.
;
;  WHY THIS IS NOT A PHANTOM. Presence is address-ACK, never idle lines: a
;  bus with pull-ups and no device rests high, the address goes out, nobody
;  pulls ACK low, S.ERR sets, and this returns #I2C_NACK. The RP2350's
;  phantom device came from reading a healthy-bus status as a hit; here the
;  only thing read as a hit is a driven ACK. (Still owed silicon - a read
;  probe can disturb a write-only device, and i2cdetect uses a write probe
;  for part of its range. A future `i2c probe` may want both modes; this
;  one read-probes, which is safe for the common sensor/EEPROM case and is
;  what -r on i2cdetect does.)
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i I2cProbe(addr.i)
  Define b.i
  b = 0
  ProcedureReturn I2cXfer(addr, @b, 1, 1)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  I2cTimeoutCount() - wedged-bus timeouts since boot. Non-zero points at
;  wiring or pull-ups rather than a missing chip.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i I2cTimeoutCount()
  ProcedureReturn i2c_timeouts
EndProcedure
